  - name: 'AscendCAD Rocket: Avionics (install inside a bay)'
    description: Guidance, range safety, telemetry and power boxes. Install them inside an avionics bay or interstage.
    parts:
    - title: Flight Computer (triple-redundant)
      image: {fileID: 0}
      shortDescription: Three processors vote on every decision; runs guidance, sequencing and engine control.
      purpose: 'Runs guidance, navigation and control software, and sequences everything from engine start to stage separation and payload release.


        Three processors vote on every decision; runs guidance, sequencing and engine control. Installs inside a bay, interstage or satellite structure.'
      specs: 'AscendCAD section: Avionics (install inside a bay) > Guidance & Navigation

        Mass: 12 kg

        Power draw: 60 W (100% of the time)

        Volume: 15 L'
      cost: '$650,000 (estimate). Where the numbers come from: est..'
      history: The Saturn V's Launch Vehicle Digital Computer in the 1960s already used triple redundancy; modern rockets still use the same voting idea.
    - title: Navigation IMU (ring-laser)
      image: {fileID: 0}
      shortDescription: 'Navigation-grade gyros and accelerometers: knows attitude and velocity without outside help.'
      purpose: 'Navigation-grade gyros and accelerometers: knows attitude and velocity without outside help. Installs inside a bay, interstage or satellite structure.'
      specs: 'AscendCAD section: Avionics (install inside a bay) > Guidance & Navigation

        Mass: 9 kg

