  - name: 'AscendCAD: Launch Pads'
    description: Launch pads and support vehicles. Bought once, kept forever. Each has limits on rocket height, width and mass.
    parts:
    - title: Basic Launch Mount
      image: {fileID: -6941893666776508460, guid: 975e18d1435cc8724a8bedb32515d1a1, type: 3}
      shortDescription: Government-provided concrete pad with a hold-down clamp and a small umbilical mast.
      purpose: Government-provided concrete pad with a hold-down clamp and a small umbilical mast. Fine for small launchers like Electron. Buy it once on the AscendCAD Launch Pad tab and choose it for any compatible rocket.
      specs: 'Type: Launch pad

        Max rocket height: 40 m

        Max rocket width: 6 m

        Max liftoff mass: 150 t'
      cost: 'Free: provided by the government. Where the numbers come from: Electron: 18 m tall, 1.2 m wide, ~13 t (Rocket Lab). Limits est..'
      history: Launch infrastructure in AscendCAD.
    - title: Gantry Launch Pad
      image: {fileID: -4919171722757787144, guid: 3856d1d91a3a38ee29789496f9bcb958, type: 3}
      shortDescription: Fixed pad with a service gantry tower, flame trench and propellant farm.
      purpose: Fixed pad with a service gantry tower, flame trench and propellant farm. The rocket is erected and serviced right at the pad. Medium rockets up to Falcon 9 class. Buy it once on the AscendCAD Launch Pad tab and choose it for any compatible rocket.
      specs: 'Type: Launch pad

        Max rocket height: 80 m

        Max rocket width: 14 m

        Max liftoff mass: 1,300 t'
      cost: '$150,000,000 (estimate). Where the numbers come from: Falcon 9: ~70 m tall, 3.7 m wide, ~550 t. Limits and cost est..'
      history: Launch infrastructure in AscendCAD.
    - title: Mobile Launcher
      image: {fileID: -852393517329727934, guid: 2d79cd3f3acb1b000bad5e520aa297e7, type: 3}
      shortDescription: Two-story launch platform with a ~116 m umbilical tower.
      purpose: Two-story launch platform with a ~116 m umbilical tower. The rocket is stacked on it indoors, then the Crawler carries both to the pad. Built for super-heavy rockets. Buy it once on the AscendCAD Launch Pad tab and choose it for any compatible rocket.
      specs: 'Type: Launch pad

        Max rocket height: 125 m

        Max rocket width: 36 m

        Max liftoff mass: 3,600 t

        Needs: Crawler-Transporter'
      cost: '$900,000,000 (estimate). Where the numbers come from: NASA: SLS Mobile Launcher tower ~116 m. Mass, limits and cost est..'
      history: Launch infrastructure in AscendCAD.
    - title: Crawler-Transporter
      image: {fileID: 7666178372080435337, guid: ec6cf5a89b0f864379ccd29ebd7a009e, type: 3}
      shortDescription: Tracked giant that carries the Mobile Launcher and its rocket to the pad at about 1.6 km/h.
      purpose: Tracked giant that carries the Mobile Launcher and its rocket to the pad at about 1.6 km/h. Required to use the Mobile Launcher. Buy it once on the AscendCAD Launch Pad tab and choose it for any compatible rocket.
      specs: 'Type: Support vehicle

        Carries up to: 8,165 t (rocket + launcher)'
      cost: '$150,000,000 (estimate). Where the numbers come from: NASA: Crawler-Transporter 2 upgraded to ~8,165 t capacity. Cost est..'
      history: Launch infrastructure in AscendCAD.
    - title: CanSat Launch Rail
      image: {fileID: 0}
      shortDescription: 6 m steel launch rail on a tripod, with a blast plate and a wired launch controller.
      purpose: 6 m steel launch rail on a tripod, with a blast plate and a wired launch controller. Small sounding rockets slide up the rail until they are fast enough for their fins to keep them straight. Only for hobby / CanSat rockets. Buy it once on the AscendCAD Launch Pad tab and choose it for any compatible rocket.
      specs: 'Type: Launch pad

        Max rocket height: 5 m

        Max rocket width: 0.3 m

        Max liftoff mass: 0 t'
      cost: '$6,000 (estimate). Where the numbers come from: Typical high-power rocketry 1515 rail (~6 m), as used at CanSat competitions. Limits and cost est..'
      history: Launch infrastructure in AscendCAD.
  - name: 'AscendCAD Rocket: Sounding Rockets (CanSat)'
    description: AscendCAD parts.
    parts:
    - title: "J310 Hobby Motor Section \xD80.1 m"
      image: {fileID: 0}
      shortDescription: "High-power hobby rocket motor in its mount tube (J class, about 560 N\xB7s)."
      purpose: "High-power hobby rocket motor in its mount tube (J class, about 560 N\xB7s). Lifts a CanSat rocket to roughly 1 km. Once lit it can't be switched off. Put it at the bottom. Stacks on top of or under other parts."
      specs: "AscendCAD section: Sounding Rockets (CanSat) > Hobby Motors\nMass: 750 g full, 450 g empty\nPropellant: 300 g solid\nThrust: 0 kN sea level, 0 kN vacuum\nSpecific impulse: 190 s sea level, 200 s vacuum\nBurn time: 2 s\nSize: \xD80.1 m x 0.45 m"
      cost: "$300 (estimate). Where the numbers come from: Typical J-class reload: ~560 N\xB7s total impulse, Isp ~190 s; price est.."
      history: Part of the AscendCAD catalog (Sounding Rockets (CanSat) > Hobby Motors). Figures follow the real hardware where published; anything marked estimate is an engineering guess.
    - title: "K420 Hobby Motor Section \xD80.1 m"
      image: {fileID: 0}
      shortDescription: "Bigger hobby motor (K class, about 930 N\xB7s)."
      purpose: "Bigger hobby motor (K class, about 930 N\xB7s). Flies higher, so the CanSat falls for longer. Stacks on top of or under other parts."
      specs: "AscendCAD section: Sounding Rockets (CanSat) > Hobby Motors\nMass: 1.1 kg full, 600 g empty\nPropellant: 500 g solid\nThrust: 0 kN sea level, 0 kN vacuum\nSpecific impulse: 190 s sea level, 200 s vacuum\nBurn time: 2 s\nSize: \xD80.1 m x 0.6 m"
      cost: "$450 (estimate). Where the numbers come from: Typical K-class reload: ~930 N\xB7s, Isp ~190 s; price est.."
      history: Part of the AscendCAD catalog (Sounding Rockets (CanSat) > Hobby Motors). Figures follow the real hardware where published; anything marked estimate is an engineering guess.
    - title: "Body Tube \xD80.1 m (avionics inside)"
      image: {fileID: 0}
      shortDescription: Fiberglass tube that forms the rocket's body.
      purpose: Fiberglass tube that forms the rocket's body. The hobby avionics go inside it. Stacks on top of or under other parts.
      specs: "AscendCAD section: Sounding Rockets (CanSat) > Airframe\nMass: 350 g\nSize: \xD80.1 m x 0.6 m\nRoom inside: 3 L"
      cost: '$120 (estimate). Where the numbers come from: est..'
      history: Part of the AscendCAD catalog (Sounding Rockets (CanSat) > Airframe). Figures follow the real hardware where published; anything marked estimate is an engineering guess.
    - title: "CanSat Payload Bay \xD80.1 m"
      image: {fileID: 0}
      shortDescription: Tube section that holds the CanSat.
      purpose: Tube section that holds the CanSat. At the top of the flight the nose pops off and the CanSat is pushed out. Stacks on top of or under other parts.
      specs: "AscendCAD section: Sounding Rockets (CanSat) > Airframe\nMass: 250 g\nSize: \xD80.1 m x 0.35 m\nMax payload: 2 kg\nUsable space inside: \xD80.09 m x 0.3 m"
      cost: '$150 (estimate). Where the numbers come from: est..'
      history: Part of the AscendCAD catalog (Sounding Rockets (CanSat) > Airframe). Figures follow the real hardware where published; anything marked estimate is an engineering guess.
    - title: "Nose Cone \xD80.1 m"
      image: {fileID: 0}
      shortDescription: Pointy plastic nose so the air flows smoothly around the rocket.
      purpose: 'Reduces drag and aerodynamic heating at the front of the vehicle. On most orbital rockets, the payload fairing itself forms the nose cone.


        Pointy plastic nose so the air flows smoothly around the rocket. Stacks on top of or under other parts.'
      specs: "AscendCAD section: Sounding Rockets (CanSat) > Airframe\nMass: 150 g\nSize: \xD80.1 m x 0.3 m"
      cost: '$60 (estimate). Where the numbers come from: est..'
      history: Nose-cone shapes were studied heavily for supersonic missiles in the 1940s and 50s; the low-drag von Karman ogive is still a favorite.
    - title: Hobby Rocket Fin
      image: {fileID: 0}
      shortDescription: Keeps a small rocket flying straight, like the feathers on an arrow.
      purpose: Keeps a small rocket flying straight, like the feathers on an arrow. Put 3 or 4 near the bottom. Mounts on the side or face of a part (symmetry works).
      specs: 'AscendCAD section: Sounding Rockets (CanSat) > Airframe

        Mass: 50 g

        Size: 0.004 x 0.12 x 0.1 m'
      cost: '$30 (estimate). Where the numbers come from: est..'
      history: Part of the AscendCAD catalog (Sounding Rockets (CanSat) > Airframe). Figures follow the real hardware where published; anything marked estimate is an engineering guess.
